<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  POV one-pager template.

  This file is the answer to claim 3: two POVs for two customers have to look
  like the same product, and "both satisfy rule 24" does not get you there.
  Two documents can each honour "whitespace is functional" and share no visual
  DNA at all. The type scale, the colour, the section order and the diagram
  treatment live HERE so they are the same every time, rather than being
  reconstructed from the model's taste on the day.

  Replace every {{PLACEHOLDER}}. Do not restyle. If a customer needs a
  different look, copy this file, change it, and point POV_TEMPLATE_PATH at
  the copy — that is what the override is for, and it is better than editing
  the shipped one because the shipped one is what makes two POVs match.

  Rule references in the comments are to specs/POV-SPEC.md. They mark the
  elements whose POSITION is load-bearing; moving them breaks the rule, not
  just the look.

  No date stamp anywhere, by rule 25.
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{CUSTOMER_NAME}} — A Perspective</title>
<style>
  @page { size: letter; margin: 0.5in; }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    font-family: "Inter", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
    color: #151515; margin: 0; padding: 0.45in 0.55in;
    font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.5; max-width: 8.5in;
  }
  h1 { font-size: 19pt; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 2pt; letter-spacing: -0.4pt; }
  .sub { color: #6a6e73; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0 0 16pt; }
  h2 {
    font-size: 8.5pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1.1pt;
    color: #6a6e73; margin: 15pt 0 5pt; font-weight: 700;
  }
  p { margin: 0 0 8pt; }
  strong { font-weight: 700; }
  .callout {
    border-left: 4px solid #1f4e79; background: #eef3f8;
    padding: 11pt 14pt; margin: 14pt 0;
  }
  .callout p { margin: 0; font-size: 11.5pt; line-height: 1.45; }
  .bridge {
    display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: 10pt;
    margin: 14pt 0; text-align: center;
  }
  .bridge div {
    flex: 1; border: 1px solid #d2d2d2; border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 9pt 7pt; font-size: 9pt; line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .bridge .mid {
    background: #151515; color: #fff; border-color: #151515;
    font-weight: 700; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    flex: 0 0 24%;
  }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 6pt 0; font-size: 9.5pt; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 5pt 8pt 5pt 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #e8e8e8; vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: #6a6e73; font-weight: 700; font-size: 8pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.6pt; }
  .tradeoff { color: #4d5258; font-style: italic; font-size: 9.5pt; }
  .cta { margin-top: 16pt; padding-top: 9pt; border-top: 2px solid #151515; text-align: right; }
  .cta strong { font-size: 11pt; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <!-- Top-left entry point, by rule 21. The headline is the thesis in one
       line, not the customer's name and not a product name. -->
  <h1>{{HEADLINE}}</h1>
  <p class="sub">A perspective for {{CUSTOMER_NAME}}</p>

  <h2>Your Environment</h2>
  <!-- The 2-3 initiatives, named by their INTERNAL program names. Bold each
       one. This paragraph is what proves you did the reading. -->
  <p>{{ENVIRONMENT}}</p>

  <h2>What We're Seeing</h2>
  <!-- Why the initiatives above are currently treated as separate, and the
       cost of that. State the situation; do not yet state the thesis. -->
  <p>{{OBSERVATION}}</p>

  <!-- The convergence callout: the most important element on the page, rule
       32. One idea. If this paragraph could be about another customer, the
       thesis is not specific enough to send. -->
  <div class="callout">
    <p>{{CONVERGENCE_THESIS}}</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Simple visual showing the product bridging both initiatives, rule 33.
       Three boxes: initiative, the thing that connects them, initiative. -->
  <div class="bridge">
    <div><strong>{{BRIDGE_LEFT_TITLE}}</strong><br>{{BRIDGE_LEFT_DETAIL}}</div>
    <div class="mid">{{BRIDGE_MIDDLE}}</div>
    <div><strong>{{BRIDGE_RIGHT_TITLE}}</strong><br>{{BRIDGE_RIGHT_DETAIL}}</div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Numbers</h2>
  <!-- Peer references. One named company and one concrete metric each, and a
       DIFFERENT reference per persona at the same account. Verify every URL
       resolves before using it. -->
  <table>
    <tr><th>Company</th><th>Situation</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>{{PEER_1_NAME}}</strong></td>
      <td>{{PEER_1_SITUATION}}</td>
      <td>{{PEER_1_OUTCOME}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>{{PEER_2_NAME}}</strong></td>
      <td>{{PEER_2_SITUATION}}</td>
      <td>{{PEER_2_OUTCOME}}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <!-- Exactly one honest limitation, rule 29. Not a disclaimer and not a
       strength in disguise. The reader is deciding whether to trust the rest
       of the page, and this is the paragraph that earns it. -->
  <p class="tradeoff">{{TRADEOFF}}</p>

  <!-- CTA bottom-right, rule 21. A question proposing a short conversation,
       not a request for a meeting to "discuss our solutions". -->
  <div class="cta">
    <strong>{{CTA}}</strong>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
